.TH VULN2 1 "2026-04-27" "CECS 378" "CECS 378 Buffer Overflow Lab"
.SH NAME
vuln2 \- Phase Omega target; deliberately vulnerable program (see phase-omega(7))
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vuln2
.B <
.I PAYLOAD
.br
.B vuln2
.B \-h | \-\-help
.br
.B vuln2
.B \-V | \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
If you have not read
.BR mainframe (7),
do so first. The attack approach is in
.BR phase-omega (7).

.B vuln2
is the second target. Its source is identical to
.BR vuln1 (1).
Two things differ. First, the linker no longer marks the stack
executable:

.RS
.nf
gcc -m32 -fno-stack-protector -no-pie
.fi
.RE

That single linker flag removal is the entire lesson of Phase Omega.
Your Phase Phi exploit will fail against this binary; explaining why
in your writeup is required.

Second, vuln2 is built with
.B \-DLEAK_LIBC
so that, in addition to the addresses of its stack buffer and saved-EIP
slot, it also prints the runtime address of
.BR printf (3)
in libc. The full leak header looks like:
.RS
.nf
[buffer @ 0x\fIADDR\fP, size N, pad K]
[saved-eip @ 0x\fIADDR\fP]
[libc-printf @ 0x\fIADDR\fP]
.fi
.RE
The libc-printf leak gives ret2libc the foothold it needs under
.B ASLR
without resorting to a separate-process leak.

It is installed setuid to user
.BR oracle (8),
who reads
.IR /srv/flag2.txt .

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary to standard output and exit 0.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the program version to standard output and exit 0.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal exit.
.TP
.B 2
Misuse (unrecognized argument).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /srv/flag2.txt
The flag file readable only by user
.BR oracle .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR phase-omega (7),
.BR mainframe (7),
.BR vuln1 (1),
.BR oracle (8),
.BR strcpy (3),
.BR execstack (8)
.SH BUGS
The program is still the bug.
